# Generating code

The `howdy create` commands write code into the project you are in, in the
shape `howdy new` gave it, with tests, and register it in the app. Four of
them add a database table with as much code around it as you ask for:

- `howdy create controller` writes the table, a record, its database
  operations and a JSON API.
- `howdy create pages` writes the same with HTML pages in place of the JSON
  API: a table of records, and forms to add, edit and delete them.
- `howdy create repo` writes the table, the record and its database
  operations, with no routes.
- `howdy create migration` writes only the table.

The rest add one thing at a time:

- `howdy create page` and `howdy create live_page` add a page to a
  controller, creating the controller if needed.
- `howdy create component` and `howdy create live_component` add a
  reusable piece of view.
- `howdy create layout` adds a layout for pages to render through.
- `howdy create email` adds an email, once the project has
  [mail](/docs/cli/add).

Run them from inside a project made with [`howdy new`](/docs/cli/new). The
commands that write a table need a database, so they do not work in one
created with `--no-database`. Whether it is SQLite or PostgreSQL is detected
from `gleam.toml`, and the generated column types follow it. Every command
takes `--dry-run` to show what it would write, and `--json` for a result a
script can read; see [The howdy command](/docs/cli/overview).

## A controller

```sh
howdy create controller users user name:string age:int
```

The first argument is the plural. It names the folder, the table, the routes
and the module of database operations. The second is the singular, which
names the record module and its type. The rest are the fields, described
below.

This creates the feature `src/<project>/features/users/`:

- `user.gleam` holds the `User` record, input validation, JSON codecs and
  the database table.
- `users.gleam` holds the database operations: `all`, `find`, `create`,
  `update` and `delete`.
- `users_controller.gleam` holds the routes.

The routes are:

- `GET /api/users` returns every user.
- `GET /api/users/:id` returns one user.
- `POST /api/users` creates a user and answers 201.
- `PUT /api/users/:id` replaces every field and answers 200.
- `DELETE /api/users/:id` deletes a user and answers 204.

The command also writes:

- `migrations/create_users.gleam`, a migration that creates the `users`
  table, numbered one above the highest migration in the project
- `test/<project>/features/users/users_test.gleam`, tests for the database
  operations
- `test/<project>/features/users/users_controller_test.gleam`, tests for
  the routes

Then it registers the controller in `src/<project>.gleam` and the migration
in `platform/database.gleam`, and runs `gleam format`. The migration runs the
next time the app starts, or when you run `gleam run -m tasks/migrate`.

Names of more than one word use underscores:

```sh
howdy create controller blog_posts blog_post title body:string published:bool
```

## Pages

For pages that people use rather than a JSON API:

```sh
howdy create pages users user name:string age:int
```

`howdy create pages` takes the same arguments and writes the same record,
operations, migration and tests, with the routes under `/users` and a
`pages/` folder in the feature:

- `pages/index_page.gleam` shows every user, in a table.
- `pages/show_page.gleam` shows one user.
- `pages/new_page.gleam` is the form for a new user.
- `pages/edit_page.gleam` is the form to change one.
- `pages/helpers.gleam` holds the form both use, and where a user lives.

The routes are:

- `GET /users` shows every user, in a table.
- `GET /users/new` shows the form for a new user.
- `POST /users` creates a user, then shows it.
- `GET /users/:id` shows one user.
- `GET /users/:id/edit` shows the form to change it.
- `POST /users/:id` updates the user, then shows it.
- `POST /users/:id/delete` deletes the user, then goes back to the table.

The pages are built from [Howdy UI](/docs/ui/components) and rendered on the
server as plain links and forms, so they work without JavaScript. That is
why update and delete are `POST` routes: an HTML form cannot send `PUT` or
`DELETE`. A form that fails validation comes back with the values still
filled in and an error under each field that needs fixing.

The pages render through the default layout in `src/<project>/layouts/`,
or the one `--layout` names. The first time you run it, the command also
writes the form helpers to `platform/pages.gleam` and adds the pages'
styles to `priv/public/assets/style.css`. Both happen once and serve every
set of pages, so they are yours to edit.

Pass `--api` to get the JSON controller from `howdy create controller` as
well, as `users_api_controller.gleam`, with the pages at `/users` and the
API at `/api/users`:

```sh
howdy create pages blog_posts blog_post title body published:bool --api
```

`howdy create page users <name>` adds another page to the same controller.

## A repo

For data without any routes, reached from your own pages or from other
features:

```sh
howdy create repo users user name:string email:string age:int
```

`howdy create repo` takes the same arguments and writes the same files,
except the controller and its test. It registers the migration only.

## A migration

For only the table:

```sh
howdy create migration users name:string email:string age:int
```

This writes `migrations/create_users.gleam`, adds it to the list in
`platform/database.gleam`, and nothing else.

It takes the table name alone, without a singular, so every field needs its
type here. A bare `name` would be mistaken for the singular, and the command
tells you so.

## A page

```sh
howdy create page blog about
```

`howdy create page` writes
`src/<project>/features/blog/pages/about_page.gleam`, a page ready for your
content, and adds its route to `blog_controller.gleam` beside the pages
folder: `/blog/about`, or `/blog` for a page named `index`. A test that the
page responds goes in `test/<project>/features/blog/pages/`.

If the controller does not exist it is created and registered in
`src/<project>.gleam`. If it does, the page is added to it and served under
whatever path the controller already uses. A page that already exists, or
a route the controller already has, is refused and nothing is written.

The page renders through the default layout, or the one `--layout` names:

```sh
howdy create page admin index --layout admin
```

## A live page

```sh
howdy create live_page dashboard index
```

`howdy create live_page` writes the same page with a live section: a
[Lustre server component](/docs/ui/live-components) whose model, update and
view run on the server, with the browser showing each change it sends over
a WebSocket. The controller gets two routes, the page at
`/dashboard` and its WebSocket at `/dashboard/index/live`. Everything else
is as for `howdy create page`, including `--layout`.

## A component

```sh
howdy create component price_tag
howdy create component blog post_card
```

`howdy create component` writes a view function that returns an element,
for pages to reuse, with a test that it renders. With one name it is
global, in `src/<project>/components/price_tag_component.gleam`; with a
controller first it belongs to that controller, in
`src/<project>/features/blog/components/`. The controller must exist. Pages
call `price_tag_component.view()`.

## A live component

```sh
howdy create live_component notifications
howdy create live_component blog comments
```

`howdy create live_component` writes a Lustre server component, global or
belonging to a controller in the same way. Pages put it on the page with
`notifications_component.mount()` and render through their layout's
`render_live`, which loads the script it connects with.

Its WebSocket is routed for you. A component of a controller gets
`/blog/components/comments/live` in that controller. A global component
gets `/components/notifications/live` in
`components/components_controller.gleam`, which the first global live
component creates and registers in `src/<project>.gleam`. A test that it
mounts and serves its socket is written beside the other tests.

## A layout

```sh
howdy create layout admin
```

`howdy create layout` writes `src/<project>/layouts/admin_layout.gleam`:
everything around a page's content, from the HTML document with its theme
and stylesheets to the header. It starts as a copy of the default layout
`howdy new` wrote, ready for you to change. Pages render through it with
`render`, or `render_live` when they have live sections. Pass
`--layout admin` to `create page`, `create live_page` or `create pages` to
use it.

## An email

```sh
howdy create email password_reset
```

`howdy create email` writes `src/<project>/emails/password_reset_email.gleam`,
an email written with smail, with `build(to:)` to make the message and
`preview()` to build it from sample data. The preview is added to
`emails/previews.gleam`, so the dev admin shows it under Mail and the tests
check that it can be sent. The project needs mail first, from
[`howdy add mail`](/docs/cli/add).

## Guarding with auth

Once the project has [`howdy add auth`](/docs/cli/add), `--add-auth` guards
what a command creates with Howdy Auth, so only someone signed in reaches
it, and anyone else gets 401:

```sh
howdy create controller notes note body --add-auth
howdy create pages notes note body --add-auth
howdy create page account index --add-auth
howdy create live_component notifications --add-auth
```

For `create controller` and `create pages` every route is guarded, and
with `--api` the JSON routes too. The controllers take the app's identity,
and their tests sign in. For `create page`, `create live_page` and a global
`create live_component`, the flag applies to the controller the command
creates. A controller that already exists keeps its guard, or lack of one,
and `--add-auth` is refused for one without.

## Fields

Fields are `name:type` pairs. The types are `string`, `int`, `float` and
`bool`. For `create controller`, `create pages` and `create repo` a bare
name is a string, so `title` means `title:string`.

Every record also gets an integer `id`, so do not list one. Give at least
one field, and give each field once.

## Names

The plural, the singular and the field names follow the same rule as project
names: lowercase letters, digits and underscores, starting with a letter.

The plural and the singular must differ, because they name two modules in
one folder. Names that would not compile or would break the generated SQL
are refused with a message saying why: Gleam keywords, SQL keywords, names
of modules the generated code imports, and singulars that would shadow a
built-in Gleam type, such as `list`.

## Your code is safe

Existing files are never overwritten. If any file the command would write
already exists, it stops and writes nothing. The files shared by every set
of pages are the exception: when they exist they are kept as they are, and
the rest is written.

Registering a controller, pages, a component's socket or a migration means
editing `src/<project>.gleam` and `platform/database.gleam`. If either no
longer has the shape `howdy new` gave it, it is left alone, and the command
prints what to add by hand, as `manual_steps` with `--json`.

## Flags

- `--api`, for `create pages` only: also create the JSON controller under
  `/api/<plural>`.
- `--layout <name>`, for `create page`, `create live_page` and
  `create pages`: render through this layout from `src/<project>/layouts/`
  instead of the default.
- `--add-auth`: guard the routes with Howdy Auth; needs `howdy add auth`.
- `--dry-run`: show which files would be created and modified, without
  changing the project.
- `--json`: print the result as one JSON object: the files created and
  modified, steps left to do by hand, and what to do next.
- `--plain`: print progress as plain lines instead of animating.
